    "clusterIssue": "Lockwire / vascular complication",
    "commentOriginal": "Múltiples intentos de posicionamiento del marcapasos transitorio por acceso venoso\nfemoral que resultaron fallidos, por lo que se decide realizar acceso yugular derecho,\nlogrando posicionamiento exitoso en ventrículo derecho.\nSe avanza la válvula montada en el sistema de delivery. El operador decide no utilizar\nintroductor, realizándose la técnica sheathless. Durante el procedimiento se presenta\ndificultad al momento de exteriorizar la válvula desde el subhandle ya dentro de la\npaciente. Posterior al cruce del arco aórtico, se evidencia angiográficamente que el\nlockwire de la válvula se encontraba fuera del cono distal.\nSe intenta posicionar la válvula para implante; sin embargo, esta no logra avanzar\nadecuadamente, por lo que se decide retirar parcialmente el sistema con la válvula aún\nmontada hasta la aorta descendente. Se indica al operador retirar el lockwire hasta la\nprimera sutura radiopaca de la válvula.\nPosteriormente, el médico decide realizar predilatación por acceso femoral izquierdo\nutilizando guía Safari y balón VACS III 20x40 mm, obteniéndose un buen resultado. Tras\nla dilatación se continúa con el implante valvular, expandiendo la válvula con 16 ml (-1 ml\ndel volumen nominal). El implante resulta exitoso, quedando la válvula bien posicionada\nen el anillo aórtico.\nSe solicita evaluación ecográfica intraoperatoria de la aorta, sin evidenciarse disección\naórtica ni derrame pericárdico.\nDurante el retiro del sistema de liberación valvular se presenta hemorragia a nivel del\nacceso femoral izquierdo. La paciente desarrolla disección vascular, la cual es manejada\ninicialmente con balón MeCross PTA y posteriormente con implante definitivo de stent\nBeGraft 8.0 x 37 mm.\n\nLATAM | 2025-13 AUG 2026\nCONFIDENTIAL - INTERNAL USE\nCONTENTS\nCASE DIRECTORY\nPAGE 31",
    "commentEn": "Multiple attempts to position the temporary pacemaker through femoral venous access were unsuccessful, so right\njugular access was used and successful positioning in the right ventricle was achieved.\nThe valve mounted on the delivery system was advanced. The operator decided not to use an introducer and performed\na sheathless technique. During the procedure, difficulty was encountered while externalizing the valve from the\nsubhandle inside the patient. After crossing the aortic arch, angiography showed that the valve lockwire was outside the\ndistal cone.\nAn attempt was made to position the valve for implantation; however, it could not be advanced adequately. The system,\nwith the valve still mounted, was therefore partially withdrawn to the descending aorta. The operator was instructed to\nwithdraw the lockwire to the first radiopaque valve suture.\nThe physician then decided to perform predilatation through the left femoral access using a Safari guidewire and a 20 x\n40 mm VACS III balloon, with a good result. Valve implantation then continued, expanding the valve with 16 mL (1 mL\nbelow nominal volume). The implant was successful and the valve was well positioned in the aortic annulus.\nIntraoperative echocardiographic assessment of the aorta showed no aortic dissection or pericardial effusion. During\nremoval of the valve delivery system, bleeding occurred at the left femoral access. The patient developed a vascular\ndissection, initially managed with a MeCross PTA balloon and subsequently with definitive implantation of an 8.0 x 37\nmm BeGraft stent.",
    "commentLang": "es",
    "comment": "Multiple attempts to position the temporary pacemaker through femoral venous access were unsuccessful, so right\njugular access was used and successful positioning in the right ventricle was achieved.\nThe valve mounted on the delivery system was advanced. The operator decided not to use an introducer and performed\na sheathless technique. During the procedure, difficulty was encountered while externalizing the valve from the\nsubhandle inside the patient. After crossing the aortic arch, angiography showed that the valve lockwire was outside the\ndistal cone.\nAn attempt was made to position the valve for implantation; however, it could not be advanced adequately. The system,\nwith the valve still mounted, was therefore partially withdrawn to the descending aorta. The operator was instructed to\nwithdraw the lockwire to the first radiopaque valve suture.\nThe physician then decided to perform predilatation through the left femoral access using a Safari guidewire and a 20 x\n40 mm VACS III balloon, with a good result. Valve implantation then continued, expanding the valve with 16 mL (1 mL\nbelow nominal volume). The implant was successful and the valve was well positioned in the aortic annulus.\nIntraoperative echocardiographic assessment of the aorta showed no aortic dissection or pericardial effusion. During\nremoval of the valve delivery system, bleeding occurred at the left femoral access. The patient developed a vascular\ndissection, initially managed with a MeCross PTA balloon and subsequently with definitive implantation of an 8.0 x 37\nmm BeGraft stent.",
    "source": "Consolidated LATAM Problem Report 2025-2026 (PDF 57f2)",
