    "clusterIssue": "Failure to advance / change of access",
    "commentOriginal": "Relato do Procedimento\nO procedimento teve duração total de aproximadamente 6 horas e 30 minutos.\nApós mais de uma hora de tentativas para progressão do sistema de entrega (delivery) pelo arco aórtico, foi realizada a\nabertura controlada da prótese para avaliação da integridade dos folhetos. Na ocasião, procedeu-se à lavagem do\ndispositivo para remoção de coágulos sanguíneos eventualmente presentes. Após confirmação, em conjunto com a\nequipe médica, de que a prótese permanecia íntegra e apta para uso, foi realizada nova montagem do sistema utilizando\no mesmo dispositivo.\nO procedimento foi iniciado por acesso femoral, com sucessivas tentativas de passagem do delivery pelo arco aórtico,\nporém sem sucesso. Ao atingir a grande curvatura, o sistema apresentava resistência significativa à progressão, mesmo\napós utilização de diferentes técnicas e dispositivos de suporte, incluindo cateter laço, fio G Curver (SuSrail) e fio\nLunderquist (Cook Medical).\nApós aproximadamente cinco horas de procedimento, a equipe médica optou pela mudança da via de acesso para\ncarótida, estratégia que permitiu a progressão adequada do sistema e a conclusão do implante com sucesso em menos\nde uma hora.\nApesar do tempo prolongado de procedimento e da complexidade anatômica encontrada, o implante foi realizado com\nsucesso, sem intercorrências com paciente ou prótese.\n\nLATAM | 2025-13 AUG 2026\nCONFIDENTIAL - INTERNAL USE\nCONTENTS\nCASE DIRECTORY\nPAGE 37",
    "commentEn": "Procedure Report\nThe procedure lasted approximately 6 hours and 30 minutes in total.\nAfter more than one hour of attempts to advance the delivery system through the aortic arch, the prosthesis was opened\nin a controlled manner to assess leaflet integrity. The device was flushed to remove any blood clots that might be\npresent. After the medical team confirmed that the prosthesis remained intact and suitable for use, the system was\nremounted using the same device.\nThe procedure began through femoral access, with repeated unsuccessful attempts to pass the delivery system through\nthe aortic arch. At the greater curvature, the system showed significant resistance to advancement despite the use of\ndifferent support techniques and devices, including a snare catheter, a G Curver (SuSrail) guidewire, and a Lunderquist\nguidewire (Cook Medical).\nAfter approximately five hours, the medical team changed the access route to the carotid artery. This allowed adequate\nadvancement of the system and successful completion of the implant in less than one hour.\nDespite the prolonged procedure and the complex anatomy, the implant was completed successfully without\ncomplications involving the patient or the prosthesis.",
    "commentLang": "pt",
    "comment": "Procedure Report\nThe procedure lasted approximately 6 hours and 30 minutes in total.\nAfter more than one hour of attempts to advance the delivery system through the aortic arch, the prosthesis was opened\nin a controlled manner to assess leaflet integrity. The device was flushed to remove any blood clots that might be\npresent. After the medical team confirmed that the prosthesis remained intact and suitable for use, the system was\nremounted using the same device.\nThe procedure began through femoral access, with repeated unsuccessful attempts to pass the delivery system through\nthe aortic arch. At the greater curvature, the system showed significant resistance to advancement despite the use of\ndifferent support techniques and devices, including a snare catheter, a G Curver (SuSrail) guidewire, and a Lunderquist\nguidewire (Cook Medical).\nAfter approximately five hours, the medical team changed the access route to the carotid artery. This allowed adequate\nadvancement of the system and successful completion of the implant in less than one hour.\nDespite the prolonged procedure and the complex anatomy, the implant was completed successfully without\ncomplications involving the patient or the prosthesis.",
    "source": "Consolidated LATAM Problem Report 2025-2026 (PDF 57f2)",
    "_builtin": true
