    "commentOriginal": "Device Advancement and Positioning\nDuring the initial advancement of the device, crossing the tricuspid valve presented some difficulty. In response, the device was withdrawn back to the inferior vena cava to allow for a second attempt at crossing the tricuspid valve.\nDuring the second attempt, the tricuspid valve was crossed without complications, and the system subsequently demonstrated adequate navigability until reaching the intended implantation position.\nValve Deployment and Complication During Implantation\nDeployment was performed slowly and progressively, with multiple angiographic assessments using contrast injections during balloon inflation. At the beginning of balloon inflation, when the initial dog-bone configuration was observed, the device was seen to progressively open from its distal portion.\nAt that point, instead of continuing with complete and continuous balloon inflation, the physician activated the safety mechanism of the inflation device, resulting in a loss of inflation pressure and force. Consequently, the valve remained partially deployed and migrated toward the more proximal portion of the balloon.\nSeveral additional inflation attempts were subsequently performed using the full balloon volume. However, the device continued to demonstrate mobility, and a stable position could not be achieved through this manoeuvre.\nGiven this situation, Dr. Rivarola decided to remove the lockwire in order to allow greater mobility and manipulation of the balloon and to attempt to impact the valve in its existing position.\nAfter several attempts at device mobilization and positioning, the valve was successfully impacted and stabilized.",
